#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068} :activity/id #uuid "fdff7ccc-5a47-5748-b28e-bcf8de0682a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163326 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6276 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fb9bf51-5dff-5207-8c2c-54d18ba6afd9" :step/math-academy-id 38034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161672 :question/id #uuid "486f4292-2f50-5d89-be1a-b3c054b449e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10082"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435315}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "0828c240-f63f-5c50-a2e8-eba11979ee4c" :step/math-academy-id 38036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161673 :question/id #uuid "41713dda-2195-5bd5-b0be-941b8188cb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10083"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435314}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "ae3ab16a-d830-544c-8331-c62d1505d7dd" :step/math-academy-id 38037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161674 :question/id #uuid "0f59a118-79ce-5746-8917-6f4092dcbc6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425755}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"} :step/id #uuid "6c760552-8a9d-54b8-962b-87e6f849c3da" :step/math-academy-id 38040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163333 :tutorial/content "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6300 :tutorial/title "The Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" :step/math-academy-id 38149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163334 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6301 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean and Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" :step/math-academy-id 38150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162169}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425756} :activity/id #uuid "7fa56d66-b299-55c7-8a43-47f002ff063b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210567c7-e894-5b02-9ee8-c6893172df38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.18\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425735} {:answer/id #uuid "60c93555-b34b-575f-95b2-f38de1599831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} {:answer/id #uuid "ca20eb7d-1ae8-5c45-ab85-1bae5f9c5811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.32\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425747} {:answer/id #uuid "cce28df6-b4e2-5293-9860-139a95e35caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.24\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425748} {:answer/id #uuid "cfed1752-e352-5a6b-9e2d-7dc6a36ff6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.33\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23ca134-e512-533c-83e5-e06d13cf4c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bcb58811-e371-5576-bf66-5ecfddbfc1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143202" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ of three nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm}\\,10\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(20+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{cm},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {5}^{2}+{10}^{2})-{6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(200)-36 \\\\  & =40-36 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}≈1.15\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "26fcd0c3-24b3-5951-9b42-4462bd2fac3d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116200b8-f0e0-5d54-986b-cdb0d1f93684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425732} {:answer/id #uuid "19e33d38-75d1-531e-92cf-f39947aea28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425734} {:answer/id #uuid "3e89daf0-19fa-53f4-b72d-31d6ed1621bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425737} {:answer/id #uuid "84db3eb9-187b-5476-8ae3-4fc97bb737af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425742} {:answer/id #uuid "b7436ec0-730a-5723-89b9-c51e09d25df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425734} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa84345-5063-5b45-a9a8-f4e3ac36fb05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27d2a840-69cf-5325-b960-367fa82827bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-143424" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is a sample of size $n=8$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =30$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=16$ and the sample size is $n=8,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{2}≈1.41.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3e79243e-67ec-56e0-9d13-5331e6398a52" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8420157b-408c-5305-9a9c-8bd7f4b5f5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159567} {:answer/id #uuid "cebb23ff-c448-538e-b197-5f1fefdfaf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159568} {:answer/id #uuid "34924901-abbb-58af-a06c-4d9ff03048f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159569} {:answer/id #uuid "4e216592-6644-5ba7-91d9-81d6f47ad0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159570} {:answer/id #uuid "560505f7-e1a5-5ea3-b88a-4c5d794b42fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186159571} :answer-field/id #uuid "c12e791e-1ac8-53c0-861b-6e4d348b043d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e399cca8-8b5c-5ff9-b9d5-05eda26fea4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143498" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ employees is drawn from a population. The population has employees with two different working hours, $38\\,\\text{h/week}$ and $40\\,\\text{h/week},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than  $0.5\\,\\text{h/week}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the employees in the population have working hours $38\\,\\text{h/week}$ and $40\\,\\text{h/week},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ employees only with the following working hours:\n\n$$\n38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,40\\,\\text{h/week}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 38+40) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(114+40) \\\\  & =\\frac{154}{4} \\\\  & =38.5\\,\\text{h/week},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot {38}^{2}+{40}^{2})-{38.5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(5\\,932)-1\\,482.25 \\\\  & =1\\,483-1\\,482.25 \\\\  & =0.75\\,{(\\text{h/week})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.75},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5\\,\\text{h/week}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.75}}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{{0.5}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{0.25}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "a723e053-b0fa-5616-b5a2-e66027d73c57"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2ebf1d-6b21-517e-8473-6b5520ced24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425730} {:answer/id #uuid "25a99cc8-8701-579f-b7ef-cb9e7b7f1e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425736} {:answer/id #uuid "55e8daa5-4432-5f7f-b7b9-25ac21b5f238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425738} {:answer/id #uuid "785ab3f2-459a-5180-8933-34aa9440618c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425740} {:answer/id #uuid "92d71196-a3a0-5ab1-b5f5-5721df71e75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425736} :answer-field/id #uuid "5943d631-1c0b-56a0-a90d-e9d31d030f54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "748a2779-e90d-59a2-9916-b0ad2a8182a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143423" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ is a sample of size $n=20$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =6$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=1$ and the sample size is $n=20,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.05}≈0.22.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c77ffddd-aed2-56b6-b329-53374af11408" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7b64bf-b6ca-57e9-aaa1-181aed3d622b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425731} {:answer/id #uuid "160f3d12-5eab-5995-9f63-e81fe88512ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "241" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425733} {:answer/id #uuid "78a69e66-0db0-56b0-9e49-ac78e00b8276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "441" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425741} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a8088-2557-53ce-b8f2-d4dcab799771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "194" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425743} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7b1ee7-ec01-58b0-9c87-f819883b152a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425745} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e022f4b-ff74-5796-8a0f-0137d56c7a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1b6912a-93b9-571b-8ea5-2d8ab50d8a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143486" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ bushes is drawn from a population. The population has bushes of two different heights, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.01\\,\\text{ft}.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bushes in the population have heights $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bushes only with the following heights:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\text{ft},\\,4\\,\\text{ft},\\,4\\,\\text{ft},\\,5\\,\\text{ft},\\,5\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(12+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5} \\\\  & =4.4\\,\\text{ft},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {4}^{2}+2\\cdot {5}^{2})-{4.4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(98)-19.36 \\\\  & =19.6-19.36 \\\\  & =0.24\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.24},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.01\\,\\text{ft}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{n}} & <0.01 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.24}}{0.01} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{{0.01}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{2\\,400} \\\\ n & >2\\,400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=2\\,401.$"} :step/id #uuid "fe9fb3ff-fe13-546b-ad08-eeb0688c4db2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425758}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425729}]}
